From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 14 Jul 2026 13:15:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] clk: cix: sky1: don't defer the whole CLKT bridge on
 partial SCMI readiness

Patch 0148 made sky1_acpi_clk_probe() defer the entire probe if even one
of the ~207 CLKT entries has not yet resolved to a live SCMI clock
(priv->pending != 0), instead of the original behavior of succeeding as
soon as any entry mapped (priv->mapped != 0, still the edge/7.0-7.1
behavior in next-patches/0003).

Returning a non-zero probe result -- including -EPROBE_DEFER -- causes
the driver core to run devres_release_all() for the device, which tears
down every clkdev entry this probe attempt created via
devm_add_action_or_reset()/sky1_clkdev_drop(). Combined with the
all-or-nothing gate, every retry of this probe walks the whole ACPI
namespace again from zero: if SCMI does not publish all ~207
scmi-clk-<id> clocks atomically in one instant, a retry that catches
even a single clock not yet registered discards all otherwise-successful
mappings and starts over. Nothing bounds how many times this can repeat,
and each iteration re-triggers deferred-probe processing for every
consumer that depends on a clkdev alias this bridge owns (cix-usbdp-phy
x4, linlondp x5 observed), producing an unbounded synchronized retry
storm rather than a bounded number of independent per-consumer defers.

This exactly reproduces on cixmini/.66: rc3 with 0148 applied never
converges past the display/USB-DP-phy probe loop, while the edge kernel
(7.0.12-cix-sky1-next), whose clk-sky1-acpi has no equivalent gate, maps
all 207 entries and succeeds in well under a second on the same
firmware/DSDT. Confirmed the two trees' clk-sky1-acpi.c are otherwise
identical (0096 is a direct port of the edge/next-patches/0003 driver);
this all-or-nothing gate is the only functional difference.

Restore "succeed once any entry mapped" as the terminal condition. Keep
0148's real error propagation (priv->error, for genuinely invalid CLKT
entries) since that part was not the regression. Keep counting
priv->pending for visibility, but only log it, don't fail on it --
consumers that still can't find their specific clock alias will get
their own -ENOENT/-EPROBE_DEFER independently from their own
devm_clk_get(), which is the existing, working, per-consumer retry path
(see patches 0154/0155/0156).

UNVERIFIED ON HARDWARE -- not yet boot-tested. Requires a supervised
test cycle (physical console watch) before deployment; see
project_o6n_dev_box_66_production_2026_07_13.md for why unattended
reboot-testing on this board is unsafe.

Upstream-Status: Pending
Signed-off-by: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/clk/cix/clk-sky1-acpi.c | 14 +++++++-------
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/clk/cix/clk-sky1-acpi.c b/drivers/clk/cix/clk-sky1-acpi.c
index a8e584d77dd1..7c1b2e6f9a11 100644
--- a/drivers/clk/cix/clk-sky1-acpi.c
+++ b/drivers/clk/cix/clk-sky1-acpi.c
@@ -179,15 +179,15 @@ static int sky1_acpi_clk_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 		return dev_err_probe(&pdev->dev, priv->error,
 				     "Invalid ACPI CLKT entry\n");
 
-	if (priv->pending)
-		return dev_err_probe(&pdev->dev, -EPROBE_DEFER,
-				     "%d ACPI CLKT entries not mapped yet\n",
-				     priv->pending);
-
 	if (priv->mapped == 0)
 		return dev_err_probe(&pdev->dev, -EPROBE_DEFER,
 				     "No SCMI clocks mapped yet\n");
 
+	if (priv->pending)
+		dev_dbg(&pdev->dev,
+			"%d ACPI CLKT entries still pending, dependent consumers will retry independently\n",
+			priv->pending);
+
 	dev_info(&pdev->dev, "Mapped %d ACPI clock lookup entries\n",
 		 priv->mapped);
 	return 0;
-- 
